      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{12}=-1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}: &={C}_{1}+2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -4 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}: &={C}_{3}+4{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+4{C}_{2}.$"
